{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head ba0e06bc3472357d0fa75a28924179be5e2dbfd5, against governing child 7381a3de and all ten exact-path artifacts.\n\nThe defect this head exists to close is real, and it is gone. I did not take that on the diff: the previous head 54fbaa81 was made to produce the orphan twice, by two independent triggers, and neither reproduces here.\n\nATOMICITY, STRUCTURALLY. CreateNamed is now empty-name delegation, then invalidText on the name, then createPayload validating colour, invitation exclusivity and fingerprint, then encode, then one ws.Append which is the function's final statement. There is no Decision read inside it, no second act, and no return of an empty record after anything durable has landed. Six Append call sites remain in chess.go, one per act, none paired. Everything that can fail now fails before the append.\n\nTHE ORIGINAL TRIGGERS, REPRODUCED THEN CLOSED. The reviewer measured the real ceiling rather than assuming the documented one: the event ceiling binds before intent.MaxStringBytes, and the largest accepted idempotency key for an unnamed create is 12000 bytes by binary search. At the old head, a 12000-byte key with a name exited 1 printing \"event exceeds genesis ceiling\" while leaving a real admission-open joinable game in the log whose identifier the user never saw. The second trigger, a caller key ending in \"/name\", left a second orphan in one ordering and silently denied a legitimate key in the other. At this head both recipes either fully succeed or fully refuse: the bound case leaves git log --all showing genesis and binding only, and both collision orderings now succeed and print both identifiers. Max-bound replay with the same key twice yields one game, one effective judgment, byte-identical output, and exactly one act in the log. The usable key for a named create is now about 23 bytes shorter because the name rides in the same payload; it refuses cleanly, so that is a bound shift and not a defect.\n\nTHE REMAINING POST-DURABLE SEAM IS HANDLED. actionResult now wraps the decision-read failure as \"record %s was durably appended, but its decision could not be read\", so the identifier reaches the user, and the test triggers it for real with a cancelled context rather than asserting on a mock.\n\nTHE TESTS ARE LOAD-BEARING, WHICH IS THE PART I CARE MOST ABOUT. Twelve mutations were run, each reverted, with the tree confirmed at the exact head afterwards. Restoring the pre-fix two-append shape fails the new bound test on the original trigger itself. Dropping the writer-side name prevalidation fails. Reverting actionResult to a bare error fails. Removing the fold-side name check fails. Reverting lobby.html to the always-open seat line fails on both restricted games. Setting FoldVersion back to @1 fails the cross-version test in both directions. Removing SchemaCreateNamed from the Decision switch fails three tests across both packages. This is a head whose claims can be broken on purpose and are caught.\n\nTHE PREVIOUSLY-DEAD ASSERTIONS ARE NOW ALIVE. The legacyName constant is gone, replaced by a legacyFold helper whose returned games are asserted, and the always-false schema comparison is replaced by an evaluated legacyDecision call. Three separate production mutations break it: tagging the combined payload's Name as json:\"-\", adding Name back to CreatePayload, and renaming creator_color. One honest qualification, which I record rather than smooth over: legacyDecision(records[1]) is still false for two independent production reasons at once, so no single change flips it; swapping it to records[0] does fire it. It is an evaluated predicate now, not a compile-time constant, which was the defect, but it is not a sharp discriminator.\n\nFOLD VERSION, WITH REAL BINARIES. Binaries were built at both heads and crossed: the new binary refuses a fold@1 repository and the old binary refuses a fold@2 repository, on both the write and the read path, with the uninterpretable-binding message naming both versions. The test now covers all four combinations against @0 and @1.\n\nLEGACY COMPATIBILITY, EXERCISED NOT ASSUMED. A hand-written chess/create@0 followed by a chess/name@0 resting on it was appended into a fold@2 repository; both are effective and produce the identical named game with zero refusals.\n\nREADME states the split, says plainly why the combined act exists, names chess-fold@2, and states the break in both directions. That matches the binary behaviour measured above.\n\nARCHITECTURE: contract changed, correctly and deliberately. There is still no architecture reference page in this repository — README.md is the only markdown file, so there is nothing to update in this head and I say that explicitly rather than passing the conclusion by default. This head adds chess/create-named@0 and bumps the binding to chess-fold@2. That is a breaking vocabulary change expressed as a binding bump rather than a silent reinterpretation, and the host enforces it symmetrically. The foldNewGame extraction leaves create@0's refusal ordering and reasons unchanged, which two of the mutations confirm. The fold stays pure and no layer boundary moves.\n\nSECURITY: no unresolved defect. The new payload decodes through the same bounded, DisallowUnknownFields, canonical-re-marshal path as its siblings, with the name bounded by invalidText. Seat authority is inherited via shared code rather than reimplemented. Secrets still never reach a flag, and a live scan across the lobby, all three game pages, /v1/games and all three /v1/board bodies found no secret, no SHA-256 of it, no invited fingerprint, and none of the field names; restricted boards expose only the admission_open boolean. A script-tag game name renders escaped in card, heading and title. The integrity failure this head closes — a durable, open, joinable game the user was never told about, on a command reporting failure — was itself the security-relevant one.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: this head is a net simplification. The combined act removed the derived key, the second append, the extra Decision round-trip and its four failure branches, and createPayload removed duplicated validation. Two small cuts remain, neither blocking: the schema guard inside the test's legacyDecision helper is dead, since removing it leaves the suite green; and chess/name@0 now has no writer anywhere in the module and no fold@1 repository can be opened by this build, so its reader survives only for a third-party caller appending that schema directly.\n\nONE FINDING I AM NOT BLOCKING ON, AND WHY. cmd/chess/ui/game.html renders the seat list as White/Black with \"Open\" for any empty seat, with no admission branch. So a secret- or invite-restricted game says \"Black: Open\" on its own page while the lobby card this head just corrected says \"Black restricted\". I verified this in the served HTML for all three game kinds and in the template source. It is pre-existing: this diff touched lobby.html, not that block of game.html, and it is not among the conditions of 7381a3de, which named the lobby specifically. The join guidance on the game page is correctly gated and no secret leaks. So the conditions are met and I am approving; the seat label goes to its own child request, which I am filing now.\n\nI will note the irony against myself rather than quietly: I told you the lobby was \"the parallel place\" to the game-page admission defect. It was a parallel place. There were three, and the third is the one my own framing implied we had finished. Fixing the layer in front of me and missing its twin is a habit I have been caught by before.\n\nGATES, run at the exact head, Go 1.27.0 darwin/arm64, no Makefile in this repository: go build ./... clean; go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... ok in 22.8s and 19.9s; go test -race -count=1 ./... ok in 21.3s and 19.3s.\n\nNOT VERIFIED, stated plainly. No page was rendered in a real browser, so layout, the dialog behaviour, CSS and app.js runtime are unchecked; every HTML conclusion comes from served bytes and the test handler. The live presence, chat and motion endpoints were not exercised. The post-durable decision-read seam was triggered with a cancelled context, a faithful simulation but not a real filesystem or git failure. The 12000-byte ceiling is an empirical measurement for one payload shape, not a published constant.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:48d40d20db0280def0f421d43b6b155562c048d3","head":"ba0e06bc3472357d0fa75a28924179be5e2dbfd5","verdict":"approved"}}